<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 622 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Tala al-Badru Alayna</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ar" dir="rtl">طلع البدر علينا</bdi> / <bdi lang="ar-Latn">Ṭalaʿ al-Badru ʿAlaynā</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of welcome that, by tradition, the people of Medina sang when the Prophet Muhammad arrived in their city.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Muslims; by tradition, the people of Medina</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Medina, present-day Saudi Arabia (by tradition)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional; by many accounts first sung at the Hijra, by another at Muhammad's return from Tabuk</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tala-al-badru-alayna">Tala al-Badru Alayna</a>&quot; is a traditional Islamic nasheed, a devotional song in Arabic. Many sources state that it was first sung when Muhammad reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> after leaving his home city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a>, as the people of the city welcomed him.</p><p>The account has been debated over a place the song names, Thaniyat al-Wadāʿ (in the song, in the plural). Some read this as a pass north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and argue that the report is geographically unlikely, since Muhammad is said to have arrived first at Qubāʾ, south of the city. Classical geographers, however, show that more than one place bore the name. The medieval geographer Yāqūt al-Ḥamawī described Thaniyat al-Wadāʿ as a pass used by travellers heading towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and other early writers also mention a pass of that name on the Meccan side of Medina. Ibn Hajar al-ʽAsqalani recorded that many authorities placed a Thaniyat al-Wadāʿ on the Mecca road, while noting another on the Syrian side, and reports cited by the historian al-Samḥūdī describe Muhammad coming from Qubāʾ and passing near a place of that name before entering the city. Some scholars take the plural to mean several passes. On this basis a number of historians regard the geographical objection as inconclusive and the reference as compatible with an approach from the south as well as from the north.</p><p>An alternative view, put forward mainly by Ibn Hajar al-ʽAsqalani, is that the song was sung to welcome Muhammad back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> after the campaign of Tabuk.</p><p>The song is widely performed today. Recorded versions include those of Oum Kalthoum, Mishary Rashid Alafasy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a> Yusuf, Yusuf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> (Cat Stevens), Mesut Kurtis, Native Deen, Raef, Maher Zain, Junaid Jamshed, Payam Azizi and Marufur Rahman. It was used in the soundtrack of Moustapha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>'s 1976 film The Message, in the scene of the Hijra, and has been taken up far beyond the Muslim world: Olivia Newton-John sang it as an interlude on Grace and Gratitude (2006), and in December 2015, as Syrian refugees first began arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, a children's choir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> sang an arrangement by Laura Hawley at a holiday concert.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of welcome in which the community greets an arrival as a light rising over them, and gives thanks for him. In the tradition it voices the joy of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> at receiving Muhammad.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Arabic</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Arabic</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">طلع البدر علينا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Talaʿa al-badru ʿalaynā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The full moon has risen over us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">من ثنيات الوداع</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">min thanīyāt al-wadāʿ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the passes of al-Wadaʿ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وجب الشكر علينا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">wajaba al-shukru ʿalaynā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>giving thanks is our duty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">ما دعا للـه داع</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">mā daʿā lillāhi dāʿ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for as long as any caller calls to God.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أيها المبعوث فينا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Ayyuhā al-mabʿūthu fīnā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O you who were sent among us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">جئت بالأمر المطاع</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">jiʾta bi-al-amri al-mutāʿ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you have come with the command to be obeyed;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">جئت شرفت المدينة</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">jiʾta sharrafta al-Madīnah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you have come, you have honoured the city:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مرحبا يا خير داع</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">marhaban yā khayra dāʿ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>welcome, O best of callers!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; the Arabic text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tala%CA%BD_al-Badru_%CA%BDAlayna" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>Source text (line 2.2): 'ﺑﺎلأمر' is typed with Arabic presentation-form characters (U+FE91, U+FE8E) for its first two letters; normalise them to the ordinary letters ب ا before display or search.</li><li>line 1.1: 'al-badr' is the full moon; in the tradition it stands for Muhammad.</li><li>line 1.2: 'Thaniyyat al-Wadaʿ' is a place name, a pass (or passes) by Medina, literally 'the passes of farewell'; kept as a name. The pass is usually placed on the north side of the city, on the road to Syria, which is why some writers doubt the traditional link with Muhammad's arrival from Mecca in 622.</li><li>line 1.4: 'mā daʿā lillāhi dāʿ' is durational ('as long as a caller calls to God'); 'dāʿ' (here and in 2.4) is the pausal form of 'dāʿin', 'one who calls, summons'.</li><li>line 2.4: 'khayra dāʿ' can also be read 'best of those who summon (to God)'.</li><li>Romanization: ALA-LC without dots under letters (so ṭ and ḥ in 'talaʿa', 'marhaban', 'mutāʿ' appear as t and h); the source is unvocalised, so the vowels are those of the usual sung reading; tāʾ marbūṭah in pausa as -ah (al-Madīnah); 'thanīyāt' follows ALA-LC's īy for the doubled yāʾ.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
